WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.760
Imagine you sit down on your couch with a brand new, really gripping thriller.

00:05.080 --> 00:06.320
Oh yeah, like a standard paperback?

00:06.540 --> 00:06.900
Exactly.

00:07.520 --> 00:11.420
You open to chapter one, and the text is in completely normal English.

00:11.520 --> 00:12.040
You start reading.

00:12.620 --> 00:12.920
Great.

00:13.300 --> 00:19.680
And, you know, the plot hooks you, the pages are flying by, and you are just entirely absorbed in the story.

00:19.980 --> 00:21.220
Like you always are with a good book.

00:21.460 --> 00:21.700
Yeah.

00:22.420 --> 00:27.660
But by the time you reach the final chapter, something basically impossible has happened.

00:28.080 --> 00:28.400
Okay.

00:28.400 --> 00:31.380
You are fluently reading an alien cipher.

00:31.680 --> 00:33.900
Like the letters look like nothing you've ever seen before.

00:34.220 --> 00:34.560
Whoa.

00:34.860 --> 00:38.640
And the really crazy part, you never once stopped to look at a decoder ring.

00:38.920 --> 00:40.180
You didn't take a class or anything?

00:40.440 --> 00:40.700
Nope.

00:41.040 --> 00:42.400
You didn't memorize flashcards.

00:42.920 --> 00:49.640
Your brain simply learned to read an entirely new writing system, and without you even noticing it was happening.

00:49.960 --> 00:52.500
I mean, that is just the ultimate cognitive heist.

00:52.800 --> 00:55.900
You're literally being hacked while you're distracted by the plot.

00:56.200 --> 00:56.740
It's wild.

00:56.740 --> 01:04.200
So today, we are taking you on a journey into a concept that completely dismantles our assumptions about how reading works.

01:04.280 --> 01:04.820
It really does.

01:05.200 --> 01:09.580
We are looking at this fascinating research paper from October 2026.

01:09.760 --> 01:12.420
It's by an independent researcher named Flykshin.

01:12.740 --> 01:12.980
Right.

01:13.160 --> 01:14.600
Titled Drifting Scripts.

01:14.600 --> 01:15.120
Exactly.

01:15.440 --> 01:22.660
And our mission for this deep dive is to explore how a publication can actually be designed to secretly alter your brain's hardware.

01:22.660 --> 01:32.300
Because, as this paper points out, whenever you look at a printed page, you know, that piece of paper is actually making two distinct commitments to you at the exact same time.

01:32.460 --> 01:34.860
Which is something we, like, almost never think about.

01:35.360 --> 01:35.580
Yeah.

01:35.580 --> 01:36.920
We totally take it for granted.

01:37.460 --> 01:39.380
The first commitment is obvious, right?

01:39.460 --> 01:40.440
The linguistic content.

01:40.860 --> 01:43.520
The words, the sentences, the story itself.

01:43.860 --> 01:44.240
Exactly.

01:44.520 --> 01:46.660
But the second commitment is the convention.

01:47.120 --> 01:51.300
The specific rules for turning that linguistic content into physical marks on a page.

01:51.800 --> 01:52.860
Like the alphabet.

01:53.560 --> 01:53.780
Yeah.

01:53.860 --> 01:54.720
Or the typeface.

01:54.720 --> 01:55.360
Yeah.

01:55.580 --> 01:58.720
We are so used to those marks that we treat them as invisible.

01:59.120 --> 02:00.460
It just feels like reality.

02:00.840 --> 02:01.620
The ink is dry.

02:02.140 --> 02:03.380
The architecture is solid.

02:03.660 --> 02:03.840
Right.

02:03.980 --> 02:11.200
So if you want to learn a new script, say you want to read Russian Cyrillic or some encrypted cipher, you have to put the book down.

02:11.720 --> 02:12.680
You have to sit at a desk.

02:13.100 --> 02:13.300
Yeah.

02:13.420 --> 02:19.660
You pull out a table of signs and you actively study the instrument before you can get back to consuming the content.

02:20.120 --> 02:23.640
And that traditional method is what linguists call productive acquisition.

02:23.640 --> 02:24.800
Productive acquisition.

02:25.100 --> 02:25.300
Okay.

02:25.740 --> 02:26.100
Right.

02:26.140 --> 02:32.540
Because you are actively, consciously trying to draw the shapes, write them out, and, you know, memorize their mapping to sounds.

02:32.860 --> 02:35.640
But what Flyxion proposes is totally different.

02:36.120 --> 02:36.320
Yeah.

02:36.380 --> 02:38.080
It's called receptive acquisition.

02:38.780 --> 02:39.080
Okay.

02:39.540 --> 02:43.740
Let's unpack this because it reminds me of that old myth about the frog in the boiling water.

02:44.360 --> 02:44.780
Oh, sure.

02:44.820 --> 02:45.140
The frog.

02:45.380 --> 02:45.620
Right.

02:45.700 --> 02:48.100
If you drop a frog in boiling water, it jumps out immediately.

02:48.420 --> 02:49.700
Because the friction's too high.

02:50.100 --> 02:50.460
Exactly.

02:50.460 --> 02:55.660
If you drop a reader into a book written in wingdings or some alien script, they close the book.

02:56.300 --> 03:01.280
But if you put the frog in cold water and slowly turn up the heat, it doesn't notice.

03:01.680 --> 03:02.420
It just adapts.

03:02.700 --> 03:02.940
Yeah.

03:03.280 --> 03:12.220
If you change a letter's shape by just 1% per page, the reader's brain adapts to the rising temperature without ever realizing it's doing hard cognitive work.

03:12.600 --> 03:15.000
And that adaptation is really the core of this paper.

03:15.000 --> 03:20.320
We are going to dismantle the mechanics of how those little black squiggles operate in your mind.

03:20.940 --> 03:21.740
That's so cool.

03:22.280 --> 03:26.740
But to do that, we have to start with the foundation of how these letters are actually swapping places, right?

03:26.900 --> 03:27.940
We have to look at the math.

03:28.320 --> 03:28.940
Oh, boy.

03:29.360 --> 03:29.800
The math.

03:30.020 --> 03:30.220
Yeah.

03:30.520 --> 03:33.360
Specifically, the illusion of visual substitution.

03:34.140 --> 03:35.060
Visual substitution.

03:35.060 --> 03:38.900
Okay, so normally when I think of a cipher, I think of hiding information.

03:39.440 --> 03:40.180
Like a secret code.

03:40.380 --> 03:44.240
Yeah, like the Caesar cipher, where you shift every letter over by three spaces.

03:44.380 --> 03:46.520
So A becomes D, B becomes E.

03:47.020 --> 03:47.200
Right.

03:47.260 --> 03:50.500
The goal is to make it totally unreadable to anyone who doesn't have the key.

03:51.180 --> 03:53.940
But here, the goal is total transparency, right?

03:54.020 --> 03:55.780
The cipher isn't trying to lock you out.

03:55.780 --> 03:58.100
No, it's trying to slowly invite you in.

03:58.560 --> 04:01.980
And to do this smoothly, you need a visual substitution cipher.

04:02.540 --> 04:05.180
Meaning you exchange letters based on how they look.

04:05.620 --> 04:06.080
Exactly.

04:06.560 --> 04:10.440
You are exchanging letters in pairs, which mathematicians call involutions.

04:10.900 --> 04:13.060
The trick is that you don't swap them randomly.

04:13.540 --> 04:13.860
Okay.

04:14.180 --> 04:17.260
You exchange a letter for one that structurally resembles it.

04:17.380 --> 04:19.160
So B swaps with P.

04:19.560 --> 04:20.780
U swaps with V.

04:21.080 --> 04:25.620
So the word public becomes B-V-P-L-I-C?

04:25.900 --> 04:26.180
Right.

04:26.540 --> 04:27.960
The P and B swap places.

04:28.540 --> 04:29.700
The U becomes a V.

04:30.120 --> 04:31.580
And the rest of the letters stay the same.

04:32.140 --> 04:39.340
Yeah, and if you look at B-V-P-L-I-C in the middle of a sentence about a, you know, a B-V-P-L-I-C park,

04:40.260 --> 04:42.480
your brain can still figure out it means public.

04:42.900 --> 04:44.840
Because the visual jump just isn't that far.

04:45.160 --> 04:45.600
Exactly.

04:45.720 --> 04:50.900
But out of curiosity, how many ways can we actually swap the 26 letters of the alphabet around?

04:51.520 --> 04:55.560
Oh, the scale of the design space is genuinely difficult to wrap your head around.

04:55.620 --> 04:56.180
Lay it on me.

04:56.740 --> 05:02.240
If you take the 26 capital letters of the Latin alphabet, the total number of possible

05:02.240 --> 05:04.480
substitutions is 26 factorial.

05:05.240 --> 05:09.280
So that's 26 times 25 times 24, all the way down to 1.

05:09.440 --> 05:09.660
Yeah.

05:10.000 --> 05:14.520
And the result is roughly 4.03 times 10 to the 26 power.

05:14.680 --> 05:15.500
Wait, what?

05:15.880 --> 05:16.100
Yeah.

05:16.300 --> 05:20.900
To put that in perspective, that is a larger number than the estimated number of stars in

05:20.900 --> 05:22.280
the observable universe.

05:22.500 --> 05:25.320
That is an astronomically large sandbox to play in.

05:25.400 --> 05:26.160
It is massive.

05:26.740 --> 05:29.280
But we aren't just making random messes in the sandbox, right?

05:29.320 --> 05:32.180
We are looking for a very specific type of swap.

05:32.500 --> 05:32.780
Right.

05:32.860 --> 05:35.400
We are looking for reciprocal substitutions.

05:35.800 --> 05:36.120
Okay.

05:36.220 --> 05:36.740
Meaning what?

05:36.740 --> 05:44.400
If A swaps with B, then B must swap with A. You can't have A become B and B become C.

05:45.080 --> 05:45.540
Oh, I see.

05:45.680 --> 05:47.800
It has to be a closed loop of 2.

05:48.200 --> 05:48.720
Exactly.

05:49.220 --> 05:53.420
And the beauty of a reciprocal cipher is that it acts as its own decoder ring.

05:53.420 --> 05:58.180
So if you run the cipher a second time on the encrypted text, you just get the original

05:58.180 --> 05:59.780
English text back perfectly.

06:00.040 --> 06:00.140
Yeah.

06:00.700 --> 06:04.100
But that constraint has to shrink the pool of possibilities, right?

06:04.780 --> 06:05.580
It must.

06:06.180 --> 06:07.320
It shrinks it drastically.

06:07.900 --> 06:19.680
But it still leaves us with exactly 532,985,208,200,576 reciprocal alphabets.

06:19.680 --> 06:23.640
So about 5.3 times 10 to the 14th.

06:24.420 --> 06:27.460
Over 500 trillion ways to pair up the letters.

06:28.340 --> 06:28.500
Yeah.

06:29.020 --> 06:30.680
So the challenge becomes...

06:31.340 --> 06:35.660
Out of half a quadrillion options, how do you find the single mathematically optimal pairing?

06:35.820 --> 06:36.040
Right.

06:36.480 --> 06:41.360
Where every single letter looks as much like its partner as possible.

06:41.660 --> 06:43.580
You definitely can't do that by eye.

06:43.700 --> 06:44.040
No way.

06:44.440 --> 06:45.760
You have to bring in a computer.

06:46.100 --> 06:46.400
Right.

06:46.400 --> 06:52.780
And the paper details testing four specific digital typefaces, Deja Vu Sons, Liberation

06:52.780 --> 06:57.280
Sons, Deja Vu Serif, and Text Gyre Pagela?

06:57.460 --> 06:57.640
Yeah.

06:57.780 --> 06:58.180
Those four.

06:58.600 --> 07:03.800
And they use a mathematical proxy to measure how much two letters look alike, called the

07:03.800 --> 07:05.300
Jacquard overlap measure.

07:05.760 --> 07:09.080
Let's visualize how the computer is actually doing this, because it's pretty neat.

07:09.260 --> 07:09.480
Okay.

07:09.480 --> 07:17.040
It takes a capital letter, say B, and renders it as a flat image, like a bitmap, on a digital

07:17.040 --> 07:21.020
canvas that is exactly 200 pixels wide and 200 pixels tall.

07:21.280 --> 07:22.460
Just a little black and white grid.

07:22.840 --> 07:23.220
Exactly.

07:23.800 --> 07:28.840
Then it does the same for the letter P. It literally lays the B on top of the P.

07:29.040 --> 07:29.240
Right.

07:29.240 --> 07:34.980
The Jacquard index calculates the area where the black ink of both letters intersects and

07:34.980 --> 07:38.460
divides it by the total area of ink used by both letters combined.

07:39.080 --> 07:41.780
So if the score is zero, they share no pixels.

07:42.120 --> 07:42.360
Right.

07:42.560 --> 07:45.380
And if the score is one, they are the exact same shape.

07:45.840 --> 07:46.100
Okay.

07:46.180 --> 07:50.980
So then they unleash this maximum weight matching algorithm to pair up the entire alphabet,

07:51.380 --> 07:51.640
right?

07:51.880 --> 07:52.100
Yeah.

07:52.440 --> 07:52.640
Yeah.

07:53.060 --> 07:57.620
Forcing the computer to find the combination that gives the highest total pixel overlap score

07:57.620 --> 07:58.880
for all 26 letters.

07:59.240 --> 08:04.620
But wait, if I pair B and P because they share that straight vertical line in the top curve.

08:04.800 --> 08:04.920
Yeah.

08:05.320 --> 08:09.300
And maybe I pair C and G because they're both circles with a chunk missing on the right.

08:09.400 --> 08:09.500
True.

08:09.840 --> 08:12.440
What happens when the math gets down to the leftover letters?

08:12.540 --> 08:13.680
Like what happens to A?

08:14.040 --> 08:18.200
Does it get forced to pair with W just because they're the last ones picked in gym class?

08:18.580 --> 08:21.740
I mean, that is the tragic reality of global optimization algorithms.

08:21.740 --> 08:22.340
Oh no.

08:22.680 --> 08:22.820
Yeah.

08:23.420 --> 08:26.580
A pairing like B and P is visually fantastic.

08:26.580 --> 08:29.880
It has a jacquard overlap score of up to 0.52.

08:30.640 --> 08:34.120
Meaning more than half their ink occupies the exact same real estate.

08:34.280 --> 08:34.600
Exactly.

08:35.320 --> 08:38.280
But the algorithm often discards that perfect pair.

08:38.960 --> 08:39.440
Why?

08:39.920 --> 08:42.400
Because it is forced to pair every single letter.

08:43.100 --> 08:47.140
Keeping B and P together might mean the only leftover options for the rest of the alphabet

08:47.140 --> 08:49.460
result in a terrible overall score.

08:49.460 --> 08:51.020
Oh, that makes sense.

08:51.620 --> 08:57.800
So in three of the tested fonts, the algorithm breaks up B and P and pairs B with S.

08:58.420 --> 08:59.920
B and S.

09:00.300 --> 09:01.380
That looks nothing alike.

09:01.620 --> 09:01.960
I know.

09:02.280 --> 09:08.840
In the fourth font, it pairs B with R. And the letter A gets stuck with W across the board

09:08.840 --> 09:10.920
with abysmal overlap scores.

09:10.920 --> 09:17.040
So a perfect mathematical swap across an entire system totally clashes with a, you know, a good

09:17.040 --> 09:20.060
enough for the human eye swap on an individual basis.

09:20.260 --> 09:20.500
Totally.

09:20.920 --> 09:25.020
The computer is prioritizing the whole, even if it makes some individual pairs completely

09:25.020 --> 09:26.880
unrecognizable to a reader.

09:27.400 --> 09:33.340
And this exposes a profound flaw in using pixel overlap to predict human perception, doesn't

09:33.340 --> 09:33.500
it?

09:33.580 --> 09:34.400
It really does.

09:35.100 --> 09:37.400
Think about the letters O and Q.

09:37.660 --> 09:37.940
Okay.

09:37.940 --> 09:40.780
In every font tested, they are the strongest pair.

09:41.140 --> 09:42.980
They score up to 92% overlap.

09:43.140 --> 09:43.280
Wow.

09:43.580 --> 09:46.600
The computer loves them because a Q is just an O with a little tail.

09:47.060 --> 09:51.700
They occupy the exact same circular geometry on that 200 by 200 canvas.

09:51.940 --> 09:52.180
Right.

09:52.580 --> 09:58.900
But consider the letters U and V. Humans universally agree that U and V look related.

09:59.680 --> 10:00.040
Oh, yeah.

10:00.660 --> 10:01.620
They share a topology.

10:02.180 --> 10:05.060
They are both open curves with two parallel ascending strokes.

10:05.560 --> 10:06.980
But, oh, I see the problem.

10:06.980 --> 10:13.020
If the computer lays them on top of each other, the U has a wide curved bowl-like bottom.

10:13.300 --> 10:13.460
Yeah.

10:13.540 --> 10:15.960
But the V plunges down to a single sharp point.

10:16.280 --> 10:18.720
So the pixels at the bottom are completely misaligned.

10:18.960 --> 10:19.480
Exactly.

10:19.860 --> 10:24.660
The computer just sees empty white space where the bowl should be and ink where the point

10:24.660 --> 10:24.940
is.

10:25.380 --> 10:26.440
So what's their score?

10:27.000 --> 10:31.040
The Jaccard Index gives U and V a miserable 20% overlap.

10:31.220 --> 10:32.240
That's terrible.

10:32.240 --> 10:35.740
The algorithm genuinely believes they look nothing alike.

10:36.320 --> 10:38.180
And we see the same failure with M and W.

10:38.380 --> 10:38.920
Oh, right.

10:39.120 --> 10:41.700
To a human, a W is literally just an upside-down M.

10:42.240 --> 10:45.360
They're deeply related by a horizontal reflection.

10:45.360 --> 10:51.840
But because pixel overlap relies on coincidence of area, like ink touching ink in the exact

10:51.840 --> 10:55.600
same coordinate space, it completely misses this structural relationship.

10:56.240 --> 10:56.340
Yeah.

10:56.400 --> 11:02.020
The pixels of an upright M and an upside-down W almost never touch, which means the mathematically

11:02.020 --> 11:06.260
optimal pairing is heavily dependent on the specific typeface you are using.

11:06.520 --> 11:06.900
Absolutely.

11:07.400 --> 11:12.300
The paper actually points out that only six out of the 13 optimal pairs survive across all

11:12.300 --> 11:13.680
four of those tested fonts.

11:13.680 --> 11:15.180
Wait, really?

11:15.860 --> 11:20.040
So if you design a cipher that works perfectly in Times New Roman and then you change the

11:20.040 --> 11:24.500
font to Arial, you might completely break the visual logic of the reading experience.

11:24.620 --> 11:24.820
Yes.

11:25.260 --> 11:29.560
Resemblance of a substitution cipher is not an inherent property of the alphabet itself.

11:30.280 --> 11:35.020
It is a fragile property of the alphabet interacting with a specific typeface.

11:35.320 --> 11:37.560
So what does this all mean?

11:37.720 --> 11:43.080
It means if measuring pixels on a digital grid fails to explain how easily we learn these

11:43.080 --> 11:46.500
shapes, we have to abandon the geometry of the computer.

11:47.260 --> 11:49.700
We have to dive into the biology of the reader's brain.

11:49.960 --> 11:50.300
Exactly.

11:50.680 --> 11:54.560
And stepping into the brain brings us to one of the most fundamental concepts in all of

11:54.560 --> 11:55.120
linguistics.

11:55.440 --> 11:55.700
Oh.

11:56.040 --> 11:57.280
The arbitrariness of the sign.

11:57.500 --> 11:57.900
Yes.

11:58.360 --> 12:03.060
This goes back to structuralist titans like Ferdinand de Saussure and Charles Sanders Pierce.

12:03.060 --> 12:03.540
Right.

12:03.900 --> 12:11.000
The core idea is that there is absolutely no necessary inherent reason why the visual shape

12:11.000 --> 12:14.100
of a B makes a B sound.

12:14.320 --> 12:14.980
None at all.

12:15.420 --> 12:17.200
The shape is a completely arbitrary convention.

12:17.680 --> 12:17.900
Right.

12:17.960 --> 12:22.600
There is no physical law of the universe that says two bumps on a vertical line must represent

12:22.600 --> 12:23.980
that sound you make with your lips.

12:24.220 --> 12:27.100
A language can change its script by sheer political decree.

12:27.420 --> 12:27.740
Really?

12:27.740 --> 12:28.220
Yeah.

12:28.440 --> 12:29.940
Look at Turkey in 1928.

12:30.320 --> 12:35.900
They replaced their centuries-old Arabic-based script with a Latin-based one practically overnight.

12:36.160 --> 12:36.440
Wow.

12:36.780 --> 12:41.220
The words sounded the same, but the visual representations changed entirely.

12:41.820 --> 12:45.080
The visual substitution cipher proves this arbitrariness.

12:45.500 --> 12:51.100
You can swap B for P, and as long as the reader understands the new rule, the language still

12:51.100 --> 12:52.080
functions perfectly.

12:52.440 --> 12:52.820
Exactly.

12:52.820 --> 12:54.080
But hold on.

12:54.280 --> 12:58.220
If the link between shape and sound is entirely arbitrary, does that mean we can just invent

12:58.220 --> 12:59.200
any shapes we want?

12:59.620 --> 12:59.980
What do you mean?

13:00.720 --> 13:06.340
Like, could I draw a picture of a little bicycle, assign it the sound S, and expect people to

13:06.340 --> 13:08.780
learn it just as easily as they learn the letter S?

13:09.080 --> 13:09.320
Ah.

13:09.980 --> 13:12.140
This is the crucial distinction the paper highlights.

13:13.140 --> 13:18.280
Arbitrariness of assignment does not guarantee equal ease of acquisition.

13:18.980 --> 13:19.680
Oh, interesting.

13:19.680 --> 13:25.120
You can assign any meaning you want to any shape, but the similarity relationships among

13:25.120 --> 13:28.580
the shapes themselves are strictly governed by human biology.

13:29.060 --> 13:31.000
So we can't just bypass our brains?

13:31.420 --> 13:31.660
Nope.

13:32.380 --> 13:33.480
The psychologist E.J.

13:33.540 --> 13:38.640
Gibson pioneered the theory of perceptual learning, and it suggests we do not learn alphabets

13:38.640 --> 13:40.980
by memorizing a holistic picture of a letter.

13:41.260 --> 13:41.700
We don't?

13:42.040 --> 13:42.260
No.

13:42.480 --> 13:44.880
We learn by discovering distinctive features.

13:44.880 --> 13:51.320
We unconsciously look for specific invariant properties that make a B different from an R.

13:52.360 --> 13:57.620
So when I was in kindergarten learning to read, I wasn't memorizing 26 unique photographs.

13:58.060 --> 13:58.720
Not at all.

13:59.340 --> 14:04.360
My brain was checking off a microscopic list of features, like, is there a vertical straight

14:04.360 --> 14:04.720
line?

14:04.880 --> 14:06.340
Is there a closed curve?

14:06.340 --> 14:07.420
Is there an intersection?

14:08.080 --> 14:11.060
That is exactly how the brain categorizes visual data.

14:11.780 --> 14:17.380
And building on that, the cognitive neuroscientist Stanislas DeHaan proposed a fascinating theory

14:17.380 --> 14:20.340
called the cultural recycling of cortical maps.

14:20.720 --> 14:21.560
That sounds intense.

14:21.960 --> 14:22.400
What does it mean?

14:22.820 --> 14:25.940
Well, reading and writing were only invented a few thousand years ago.

14:26.180 --> 14:28.180
That is an evolutionary blink of an eye.

14:28.360 --> 14:28.600
True.

14:28.600 --> 14:33.360
Evolution has not had time to build a dedicated reading center in the human brain.

14:34.080 --> 14:37.020
Instead, we recycle existing visual machinery.

14:37.420 --> 14:38.920
Like a region that already existed.

14:39.180 --> 14:41.640
Yeah, a region called the visual word form area.

14:42.100 --> 14:46.980
It originally evolved for recognizing objects, faces, and animal tracks in the natural world.

14:47.440 --> 14:48.100
That is incredible.

14:48.640 --> 14:50.460
It's like repurposing tools in a shed.

14:50.740 --> 14:55.540
We are taking an evolutionary hammer that was built to recognize the shape of a predator in

14:55.540 --> 15:01.180
the bushes, and we are forcing it to act like a magnifying glass to read 12-point Helvetica text.

15:01.820 --> 15:04.900
It works, but it's bound by the physical limits of the original tool.

15:05.200 --> 15:09.080
And those limits come with deeply ingrained biological biases, don't they?

15:09.460 --> 15:10.600
Oh, massive biases.

15:11.240 --> 15:16.360
For millions of years, our visual cortex kept us alive by generalizing across mirror images.

15:16.680 --> 15:19.180
Because in the natural world, symmetry is everywhere.

15:19.580 --> 15:19.980
Exactly.

15:20.760 --> 15:25.200
A tiger facing to the left is the exact same physical object and the exact same mortal threat

15:25.200 --> 15:26.400
as a tiger facing to the right.

15:26.940 --> 15:31.420
So your brain evolved to instantly recognize that those two mirrored images are the same entity.

15:31.740 --> 15:31.940
Yes.

15:32.500 --> 15:33.940
But then humanity invents the alphabet.

15:34.560 --> 15:38.100
And suddenly, this survival mechanism becomes a massive liability.

15:38.460 --> 15:39.080
A huge one.

15:39.740 --> 15:43.300
Because in the alphabet, a circle with a vertical stick on the left is a B.

15:43.680 --> 15:46.080
But if you move the stick to the right, it's a D.

15:46.260 --> 15:46.440
Right.

15:46.740 --> 15:49.080
If you move the stick down, it's a P.

15:49.460 --> 15:50.840
And if you flip it, it's a Q.

15:50.840 --> 15:56.320
The alphabet violently forces our brain to treat mirror images as entirely different objects.

15:56.680 --> 15:59.880
Which is why almost all children experience the mirror error when learning to read.

16:00.480 --> 16:01.800
Oh, I remember doing that.

16:01.920 --> 16:02.080
Yeah.

16:02.300 --> 16:05.900
They naturally treat B and D as the same object, writing them interchangeably.

16:06.740 --> 16:12.180
Formal schooling has to forcibly, painfully train the brain to suppress a million-year-old survival instinct.

16:12.380 --> 16:12.640
Wow.

16:13.180 --> 16:15.940
And this creates an absolutely incredible paradox for the drifting book.

16:16.480 --> 16:16.920
How so?

16:16.920 --> 16:23.280
If you design your cipher by swapping mirror-related letters, like swapping B with D or P with Q,

16:23.860 --> 16:27.640
you are deliberately recruiting the brain's most well-documented source of confusion.

16:28.100 --> 16:28.900
Oh, man.

16:29.220 --> 16:31.800
So the visual resemblance is pulling double duty.

16:32.280 --> 16:32.660
Exactly.

16:32.880 --> 16:38.580
On one hand, it's a bridge to learning, because the shapes are so structurally similar that the transition feels small.

16:38.840 --> 16:40.820
But on the other hand, it's a massive obstacle.

16:41.280 --> 16:44.740
Your brain inherently wants to blur B and D together anyway.

16:44.740 --> 16:47.500
You're poking the bear of our evolutionary biology.

16:48.080 --> 16:53.260
You are asking the brain to unlearn the very distinction that took years of childhood schooling to enforce.

16:53.920 --> 16:58.420
So the arbitrariness of the sign is completely separable from the constraints of the brain.

16:59.020 --> 16:59.320
Right.

16:59.820 --> 17:06.380
The assignment of meaning is flexible, but the neurological pathway to acquire that meaning is paved with evolutionary speed bumps.

17:06.880 --> 17:10.180
We can't force the brain to learn every transition equally fast.

17:10.180 --> 17:11.340
All right.

17:11.420 --> 17:16.300
So we understand the biological constraints, and we understand the mathematical difficulty of finding good pairs.

17:16.700 --> 17:16.800
Yeah.

17:16.840 --> 17:19.340
But how do you practically build this thing?

17:19.860 --> 17:22.000
A printed book is just a static object.

17:22.380 --> 17:24.520
How does ink on a page gradually change?

17:25.020 --> 17:25.580
Good question.

17:26.100 --> 17:33.580
The paper dissects what they call the addition, breaking a printed page down into an engine with four distinct adjustable layers.

17:33.580 --> 17:41.440
When you look at a page of text, you think you are looking at one unified thing, but you're actually looking at a composite stack.

17:41.900 --> 17:43.140
Let's break down those four layers.

17:43.480 --> 17:43.660
Okay.

17:43.820 --> 17:46.420
The first layer is the underlying linguistic text.

17:46.720 --> 17:49.900
These are the actual English words the author typed.

17:50.180 --> 17:50.760
Pretty straightforward.

17:51.360 --> 17:53.040
The second layer is the substitution.

17:53.760 --> 18:00.420
This is the mathematical rule operating on the text, dictating that a B should currently be replaced by a P.

18:01.220 --> 18:01.520
Okay.

18:01.520 --> 18:03.820
The third layer is the glyph assignment.

18:04.180 --> 18:10.400
This is the font, the physical vector shape drawn on the screen for whatever character the substitution layer hands it.

18:10.900 --> 18:11.340
And the fourth.

18:11.600 --> 18:13.160
And the fourth layer is the schedule.

18:13.620 --> 18:22.220
This is the invisible pacemaker that decides exactly how fast the substitution and the font are allowed to change as you progress through the physical pages of the book.

18:23.000 --> 18:29.200
Flyxion introduces a rule here that seems incredibly important for the digital age, called the stored text principle.

18:29.420 --> 18:30.080
Oh, this is vital.

18:30.080 --> 18:41.060
The rule states that the underlying digital file of the book, like the EPUB, the HTML, the raw data, should always constantly remain normal, unencrypted, plain text.

18:41.280 --> 18:46.500
The visual font and the display engine should be the only things doing the heavy lifting of morphing the shapes.

18:47.240 --> 18:48.460
But why does that distinction matter?

18:48.460 --> 18:55.500
I mean, if the screen looks like a bizarre alien cipher, who cares if the invisible code underneath still says A-P-P-L-E?

18:55.920 --> 18:58.600
It matters deeply for digital accessibility and utility.

18:59.240 --> 19:00.980
Think about how we interact with text today.

19:01.180 --> 19:02.240
Like searching for words?

19:02.440 --> 19:03.180
We search for keywords.

19:03.640 --> 19:04.600
We use spell checkers.

19:05.340 --> 19:10.440
Most importantly, visually impaired readers rely on screen reading software that converts text to speech.

19:10.440 --> 19:11.420
Ah, right.

19:11.820 --> 19:22.420
If you hard code the cipher into the text file, like if you actually replace the letter P with a B in the file so the word becomes V-V-P-L-I-C, all of those utilities break.

19:22.900 --> 19:24.460
A screen reader wouldn't say public.

19:24.620 --> 19:26.440
It would read out literal phonetic garbage.

19:26.740 --> 19:27.200
Exactly.

19:27.200 --> 19:33.100
By strictly enforcing the stored text principle, you preserve the semantic integrity of the document.

19:33.580 --> 19:37.440
The author's original prose is entirely intact and machine-readable.

19:37.780 --> 19:40.920
It's exactly like a digital avatar in a modern video game.

19:41.020 --> 19:42.360
Oh, that's a phenomenal analogy.

19:42.680 --> 19:42.940
Right.

19:43.020 --> 19:46.400
The stored text is the player's underlying skeletal wireframe.

19:46.700 --> 19:51.200
It's the core mathematical code that the game engine uses to handle gravity, physics, and hitboxes.

19:51.760 --> 19:51.860
Yeah.

19:51.860 --> 19:57.640
But the glyph assignment, the font, is the cosmetic costume you put over that wireframe.

19:58.300 --> 20:05.660
The game engine fundamentally knows you are a standard human character, but to the viewer, you look like a medieval knight or a sci-fi cyborg.

20:05.900 --> 20:07.520
The font is just the costume.

20:07.980 --> 20:09.300
That is perfectly stated.

20:09.800 --> 20:12.060
And statically, we already have tools that do this.

20:12.080 --> 20:12.460
We do.

20:12.700 --> 20:17.760
Yeah, the Lingo Jam cipher font, for example, takes standard text and slaps a cipher costume on it.

20:17.760 --> 20:22.040
If you type an A, it displays a runic symbol, but the computer still knows it's an A.

20:22.500 --> 20:26.080
However, this paper asks a much harder question, right?

20:26.200 --> 20:28.940
How do we make the costume change gradually?

20:29.220 --> 20:29.520
Yes.

20:29.800 --> 20:37.860
How do you take the shape of an English B and seamlessly melt it into the shape of an English P over the course of 300 pages?

20:38.200 --> 20:40.720
Because fonts are usually rigid, static files.

20:40.880 --> 20:41.080
Right.

20:41.540 --> 20:42.160
A B is a B.

20:42.500 --> 20:45.120
A font doesn't have a built-in clock or a page counter.

20:45.120 --> 20:49.400
It doesn't know it's supposed to look 10% more like a P on page 30.

20:49.780 --> 20:54.360
To achieve that gradual melt, you have to use OpenType variable fonts.

20:54.540 --> 20:55.680
OpenType variable fonts.

20:55.760 --> 20:55.880
Okay.

20:56.220 --> 21:00.060
This is a technology introduced in the OpenType 1.8 specification.

21:00.680 --> 21:04.360
Traditional fonts had separate files for regular, bold, and italic.

21:04.480 --> 21:04.700
Right.

21:04.700 --> 21:12.500
But variable fonts contain design axes to allow a single file to fluidly interpolate between extremes.

21:13.320 --> 21:14.900
Imagine a slider for font weight.

21:15.260 --> 21:20.960
You can slide it from 100 to 900, and the font calculates every intermediate thickness on the fly.

21:21.220 --> 21:29.340
So they use that same sliding mechanism, but instead of sliding from thin to bold, they create a custom axis that slides from shape A to shape B.

21:29.340 --> 21:29.860
Exactly.

21:30.620 --> 21:35.140
But there is a strict mathematical requirement for this to work called point compatibility.

21:35.820 --> 21:36.500
What does that mean?

21:36.880 --> 21:42.200
The master outlines of the two shapes must have the exact same number of structural vector points and contours.

21:42.600 --> 21:43.260
Oh, I see.

21:43.460 --> 21:51.220
You can't just morph a simple triangle into a complex snowflake unless you carefully map which point on the triangle corresponds to which point on the snowflake.

21:51.500 --> 21:51.800
Right.

21:51.900 --> 21:53.800
Otherwise, it just tangles up into a mess.

21:53.800 --> 21:54.160
Yeah.

21:54.800 --> 21:59.760
Once they are point compatible, the typesetting engine can calculate every micromillimeter of transition.

22:00.100 --> 22:02.500
But the font itself still can't count words.

22:02.700 --> 22:06.760
You can't just tell a variable font, hey, figure out where you are in the novel and adjust your slider.

22:07.120 --> 22:07.440
Correct.

22:08.080 --> 22:10.720
The font is blind to the larger structure of the book.

22:11.440 --> 22:14.360
It only renders what it is told to render at that exact moment.

22:14.900 --> 22:15.780
So what controls it?

22:16.460 --> 22:19.100
The system requires an addition builder.

22:19.660 --> 22:23.200
This is an orchestrating piece of software that pre-reads the entire manuscript.

22:23.200 --> 22:23.800
Okay.

22:24.080 --> 22:33.120
It computes the exact schedule of when every single letter should morph, annotates the text file with invisible metadata, and then hands it to a sophisticated typesetting engine.

22:33.580 --> 22:41.120
And the engine reads that metadata and dials in the exact decimal value on the variable font access for every single word on every single page.

22:41.600 --> 22:43.960
It is a staggering mechanical choreography.

22:44.820 --> 22:47.200
Okay, so we've got this beautifully orchestrated engine.

22:47.200 --> 22:55.220
The reader hits page 50, and the engine starts sliding the variable font access, gradually morphing the letter B into the letter P.

22:55.900 --> 22:56.000
Yeah.

22:56.400 --> 23:02.220
And simultaneously, because this is a reciprocal closed loop, it is morphing the letter P into the letter B.

23:02.400 --> 23:02.580
Right.

23:03.220 --> 23:05.660
And here is where the theory hits an absolute brick wall.

23:06.140 --> 23:08.420
The math encounters a catastrophic failure.

23:09.100 --> 23:09.780
Ah, yes.

23:09.780 --> 23:12.720
Let's talk about the collision on the anti-diagonal.

23:13.160 --> 23:17.660
This is one of the most elegant, if problematic, mathematical proofs in the paper.

23:18.040 --> 23:20.480
The collapse of linear exchange.

23:21.000 --> 23:22.160
Okay, walk us through it.

23:22.560 --> 23:26.380
Let's visualize the geometry of this drift in a two-dimensional shape space.

23:26.960 --> 23:30.400
You have two starting points, letter A and letter B.

23:30.620 --> 23:30.940
Got it.

23:30.940 --> 23:37.440
You're moving A to the destination shape B, and moving B to the destination shape A.

23:38.020 --> 23:41.160
If you use straightforward linear interpolation...

23:41.160 --> 23:45.840
Meaning you just draw a straight line between the start and end points and slide the vector points along that line?

23:46.080 --> 23:46.420
Exactly.

23:47.040 --> 23:49.820
If you do that, there is an unavoidable collision.

23:50.600 --> 23:51.660
Let me visualize this.

23:51.740 --> 23:55.160
It's like having two trains on the exact same track heading toward each other.

23:55.160 --> 24:01.520
The B train is heading south toward P-Ville, and the P train is heading north toward B-Town.

24:01.920 --> 24:04.440
A linear exchange guarantees a head-on collision.

24:04.540 --> 24:04.740
Right.

24:05.200 --> 24:08.020
And that collision happens at exactly the midway point.

24:08.600 --> 24:14.300
If the schedule moves smoothly from 0% to 100%, at exactly the 50% mark,

24:14.660 --> 24:19.620
the morphing shape for the original letter B and the morphing shape for the original letter P

24:19.620 --> 24:22.040
will form the exact same intermediate glyph.

24:22.080 --> 24:23.140
They intersect perfectly.

24:23.140 --> 24:25.660
The paper includes visual figures proving this.

24:26.000 --> 24:28.760
At 50%, they are pixel for pixel identical.

24:29.260 --> 24:30.920
Wait, think about what that means for the reader.

24:31.160 --> 24:32.720
I'm on page 150 of this thriller.

24:33.000 --> 24:34.420
I'm trying to figure out who the killer is.

24:34.480 --> 24:34.600
Yeah.

24:34.900 --> 24:39.520
And I encounter this weird, halfway-morphed B-P hybrid shape.

24:40.040 --> 24:43.840
But because both letters collapsed into the exact same shape,

24:43.900 --> 24:48.480
I literally have no visual way of knowing which letter the author originally typed.

24:48.740 --> 24:51.200
You have completely lost the visual distinction.

24:51.200 --> 24:57.560
What typography refers to as the margin, the smallest geometric distance required between

24:57.560 --> 25:02.740
two distinct letters for the brain to tell them apart, has dropped to absolute zero.

25:02.740 --> 25:04.520
The shapes are one and the same.

25:05.020 --> 25:08.540
At that moment of collision, the visual information is destroyed.

25:08.680 --> 25:14.240
The only tool the reader has left to decode the word is pure, top-down, contextual guessing.

25:14.240 --> 25:23.160
So, if you see the word A-hybrid-hybrid-L-E, your brain uses context to guess the hybrids represent P, spelling apple.

25:23.640 --> 25:23.900
Right.

25:24.100 --> 25:28.140
If the word is B-A-N-A-N-A, you guess the hybrid is a B.

25:28.600 --> 25:31.200
But relying purely on context is exhausting.

25:31.420 --> 25:34.360
That sounds like a miserable reading experience.

25:34.360 --> 25:40.760
We're supposedly asking the reader to effortlessly learn a new script, but halfway through the book, we completely blindfold them.

25:41.120 --> 25:42.200
Yeah, it doesn't work.

25:42.600 --> 25:45.380
How do we fix the train crash without abandoning the swap?

25:45.780 --> 25:46.940
You have to build a detour.

25:47.360 --> 25:48.460
A detour.

25:48.860 --> 25:52.360
The collision only happens because we constrained the morph to a straight line,

25:52.720 --> 25:55.660
a single dimension of change between shape A and shape B.

25:55.660 --> 26:01.920
The mathematical solution is to move outside that linear path into a third dimension in shape space.

26:02.480 --> 26:04.640
You have to go around the collision point.

26:04.800 --> 26:05.260
Precisely.

26:05.780 --> 26:12.200
Going back to the trains, we can't move them off the tracks entirely, so we have to build a tiny bridge.

26:12.720 --> 26:15.620
One train passes slightly over the other train so they don't hit.

26:15.860 --> 26:16.240
That's it.

26:16.860 --> 26:19.400
But what does a bridge look like in typography?

26:19.400 --> 26:29.300
In typographical terms, a detour means introducing a temporary artificial visual feature that doesn't exist in either the starting alphabet or the ending alphabet.

26:29.880 --> 26:30.700
Okay, like what?

26:30.960 --> 26:36.360
For example, you could add a small dot above the B and a small dot below the P.

26:36.880 --> 26:37.280
Ah.

26:38.520 --> 26:43.040
So, as the letters begin their drift, these little dots slowly fade in.

26:43.040 --> 26:52.440
Yes, they get darker and more prominent, peaking in absolute visibility right at the 50% mark where the core skeletons of the letters are otherwise identical.

26:52.920 --> 26:53.740
Oh, that's brilliant.

26:53.880 --> 26:59.140
So, the reader sees two identical halfway shapes, but one has a dot on top and one has a dot on the bottom.

26:59.700 --> 27:00.100
Exactly.

27:00.760 --> 27:05.500
So, the brain knows, okay, the top dot is the B train and the bottom dot is the P train.

27:06.140 --> 27:08.520
The dots maintain the margin of legibility.

27:08.520 --> 27:09.080
They do.

27:09.860 --> 27:16.740
And then, as the letters continue past the 50% mark and complete their journey to their final forms, the dots slowly fade away and vanish.

27:17.380 --> 27:19.620
The letters successfully bypass each other.

27:20.020 --> 27:20.260
Yeah.

27:21.000 --> 27:26.320
What is truly fascinating about this mathematical detour is that it isn't just a theoretical hack.

27:26.720 --> 27:31.180
It has a direct, profound historical analog in the real world of writing systems.

27:31.440 --> 27:32.000
Wait, really?

27:32.600 --> 27:35.800
Where have we seen temporary dots used to save legibility?

27:36.040 --> 27:37.560
In the development of the Arabic script.

27:37.560 --> 27:38.200
No way.

27:38.520 --> 27:42.000
Early Arabic was written using a skeletal script known as RASM.

27:42.640 --> 27:46.360
Many distinct Arabic letters shared the exact same base skeletal shape.

27:46.500 --> 27:47.080
Oh, I see.

27:47.440 --> 27:52.520
For instance, the letters for B, T, and THU all shared a similar structural curve.

27:53.460 --> 28:02.720
Historically, early texts left these forms completely ambiguous, relying on the reader's deep knowledge of the language and context to figure out the word, just like the 50% collision point we discussed.

28:02.720 --> 28:10.240
But as the empire expanded and non-native speakers had to read these texts, that pure contextual guessing wasn't good enough anymore.

28:10.340 --> 28:12.080
They needed a margin of legibility.

28:12.560 --> 28:13.020
Precisely.

28:13.020 --> 28:18.500
To solve the ambiguity without destroying the historical skeletons, the system introduced ijom.

28:18.700 --> 28:19.300
Dotting.

28:19.300 --> 28:20.280
Yeah, dotting.

28:20.760 --> 28:26.540
They placed specific arrangements of dots above or below the skeletal curves to distinguish letters that were otherwise identical.

28:27.020 --> 28:27.680
That is wild.

28:27.680 --> 28:37.820
A 21st century algorithm trying to solve a collision in an open-type variable font independently rediscovers a centuries-old orthographic solution used by ancient scribes.

28:38.060 --> 28:38.880
It is pretty amazing.

28:38.880 --> 28:49.160
But the trade-off is that for a huge chunk of our drifting book, the reader has to look at these weird dotted hybrid letters that don't belong to English or the alien cipher.

28:49.660 --> 28:51.960
They belong to this weird middle space.

28:52.600 --> 28:55.740
It is a necessary trade-off for a reciprocal cipher.

28:55.880 --> 28:56.060
Right.

28:56.220 --> 29:01.240
Now, you can mathematically avoid the detour entirely if you abandon reciprocal pairs.

29:01.860 --> 29:02.080
How?

29:02.080 --> 29:14.840
The paper proves that if you use a 3-cycle where A morphs into B, B morphs into C, and C morphs into A, and you arrange them in a triangle in shape space, they will never collide.

29:15.660 --> 29:17.140
Oh, that makes geometric sense.

29:17.900 --> 29:21.180
But if you do that, your cipher is no longer self-decoding.

29:21.420 --> 29:24.040
You lose that elegant mathematical symmetry.

29:24.040 --> 29:28.980
Okay, so the detour solves how the shapes physically move past each other without crashing.

29:29.220 --> 29:32.340
But that creates a massive psychological problem.

29:32.520 --> 29:33.100
Oh, absolutely.

29:33.640 --> 29:37.920
If I'm designing this book, what stops me from just changing all 26 letters on page 2?

29:38.400 --> 29:41.140
How do we pace the drift so the reader doesn't panic?

29:41.320 --> 29:42.340
Yeah, you can't just rush it.

29:42.860 --> 29:47.140
According to the paper, the schedule isn't dictated by chapter breaks or page numbers.

29:47.320 --> 29:51.800
It is entirely dictated by the statistical reality of the English language itself.

29:52.420 --> 29:53.560
By Zip's Law.

29:53.560 --> 29:55.540
Yeah, and something called the novelty load.

29:55.600 --> 29:55.840
Right.

29:56.480 --> 30:01.120
This is where the psychology of cognitive load intersects with statistical linguistics.

30:02.000 --> 30:09.300
If your goal is receptive acquisition, getting the brain to passively learn a shape, you can't base the schedule on arbitrary page counts.

30:09.740 --> 30:10.920
You must base it on exposure.

30:11.340 --> 30:17.060
A reader needs to actually lay their eyes on a specific shape a certain number of times for the brain to map it.

30:17.520 --> 30:18.540
That makes intuitive sense.

30:18.540 --> 30:28.260
If you show me a bizarre new shape for the letter A just once on page 10, and I don't see it again until page 50, I will have completely forgotten it.

30:28.380 --> 30:28.620
Totally.

30:28.900 --> 30:33.240
But if I read it 40 times in a single paragraph, my brain recognizes the pattern.

30:33.240 --> 30:37.860
The problem, though, is that letters do not appear equally in English text.

30:38.080 --> 30:38.760
Not even close.

30:39.520 --> 30:47.020
Zip's Law describes how the frequency of words and, by extension, letters in natural language follows a heavy-tailed power distribution.

30:47.340 --> 30:52.400
Meaning a few items appear constantly and many items appear almost never.

30:52.400 --> 30:54.520
Let's look at the extremes.

30:55.340 --> 31:00.960
The letter E is the most common letter in English, with a relative frequency of about 12.7%.

31:00.960 --> 31:01.380
Okay.

31:01.720 --> 31:08.500
The letter Z is one of the rarest, sitting at a frequency of roughly 0.07%.

31:08.500 --> 31:10.980
Let's make that tangible for you listening.

31:11.400 --> 31:14.260
The paper models this using a standard 80,000-word book.

31:14.480 --> 31:16.000
A typical paperback novel.

31:16.320 --> 31:16.560
Right.

31:16.840 --> 31:20.140
That's roughly 376,000 individual letters.

31:20.140 --> 31:26.320
In that entire novel, the letter E is going to show up about 47,000 times.

31:26.800 --> 31:26.920
Wow.

31:27.620 --> 31:33.160
But the letter Z, it might only appear about 278 times in the entire book.

31:33.340 --> 31:36.460
And that statistical gap is the engine of our scheduling problem.

31:36.600 --> 31:37.060
How so?

31:37.720 --> 31:40.980
Let's say we program our edition builder with a rule.

31:41.800 --> 31:47.240
The reader must be exposed to a letter 40 times before that letter is allowed to advance to its next stage of morphing.

31:47.580 --> 31:47.900
Okay.

31:47.900 --> 31:54.640
Because E appears thousands of times a chapter, it is going to blaze through its required exposures.

31:55.260 --> 31:58.940
It could finish its entire multi-stage transformation in a few pages.

31:59.140 --> 31:59.900
But Z...

31:59.900 --> 32:02.820
But Z becomes the absolute bottleneck of the entire project.

32:02.940 --> 32:03.520
Yeah, it does.

32:03.880 --> 32:11.940
If Z needs 40 exposures just to move one millimeter on the variable font slider, you might read three whole chapters before you hit 40 Zs.

32:11.940 --> 32:17.200
This brings us to a critical threshold the paper defines as the novelty load.

32:17.460 --> 32:18.780
The novelty load.

32:18.820 --> 32:19.120
Okay.

32:19.360 --> 32:19.640
The novelty load.

32:20.020 --> 32:26.880
The novelty load is the fraction of letters on any given page that are currently in an intermediate, unfamiliar morphing state.

32:27.600 --> 32:29.140
Think of it like a cognitive budget.

32:29.540 --> 32:32.060
It's like adding weights to a backpack while someone is hiking.

32:32.300 --> 32:32.700
I like that.

32:32.700 --> 32:36.860
If you slip a tiny one ounce pebble into their backpack every mile, their muscles adapt.

32:37.140 --> 32:38.480
They don't even notice the extra weight.

32:38.640 --> 32:38.760
Right.

32:39.000 --> 32:43.840
But if you suddenly drop a 10 pound brick into the bag, their knees buckle and they refuse to take another step.

32:43.940 --> 32:44.800
Perfect analogy.

32:44.800 --> 32:50.240
If you change too many common letters at once, the novelty load spikes to 100%.

32:50.240 --> 32:52.360
The page just looks like alien static.

32:52.980 --> 32:53.360
Exactly.

32:54.300 --> 32:59.080
The reader's cognitive backpack becomes overwhelmingly heavy and they abandon the book.

32:59.920 --> 33:06.800
You have to keep the temperature of the boiling water low, capping the novelty load at something manageable like 15 to 25%.

33:07.320 --> 33:11.940
But how do you balance the backpack when the letters appear at such wildly different rates?

33:11.940 --> 33:16.900
You are forced to confront the brutal reality of exposure conservation.

33:17.520 --> 33:21.120
Which means the rarest letter in the alphabet dictates the pace of the entire book.

33:21.280 --> 33:22.240
Let's go back to our math.

33:22.840 --> 33:31.760
If a complete transition from start to finish requires 200 total exposures to fully learn, say, five distinct stages of 40 exposures each,

33:32.720 --> 33:41.220
the letter Z, which only appears 278 times in the entire novel, will spend 72% of the entire book in a state of transition.

33:41.220 --> 33:43.240
72% of the book.

33:43.760 --> 33:49.980
So for three quarters of the thriller, Z is just stuck in this weird, detour-dotted, halfway state.

33:50.340 --> 33:55.060
While E might complete its entire transformation from English to alien in the first 3,000 words.

33:55.200 --> 33:56.060
That's wild.

33:56.720 --> 34:02.920
So, to prevent the novelty load from crushing the reader, the system must simulate a rare first ordering.

34:02.920 --> 34:09.100
You must start drifting the rare letters, like Z, Q, J, X, on page 1, line 1.

34:09.300 --> 34:12.480
Because they appear so infrequently, they are like those tiny pebbles.

34:12.680 --> 34:13.040
Exactly.

34:13.180 --> 34:18.540
Even if Z, Q, and X are all morphing simultaneously, they make up less than 1% of the text.

34:18.800 --> 34:21.120
They contribute almost nothing to the novelty load.

34:21.200 --> 34:23.300
The reader will barely even notice them changing.

34:23.520 --> 34:24.100
You've got it.

34:24.100 --> 34:26.860
But you have to actively hold back the common letters.

34:27.480 --> 34:30.440
E, T, A, O, I, N.

34:30.760 --> 34:35.380
They must remain completely stable in standard English until much, much later in the book.

34:35.660 --> 34:40.560
Because the moment E begins to drift, it suddenly affects 12% of every single word on the page.

34:41.260 --> 34:43.640
The novelty load rockets upward instantly.

34:44.000 --> 34:46.760
Think about how weird that reading experience would actually feel.

34:46.940 --> 34:47.640
It would be bizarre.

34:47.640 --> 34:53.240
You're reading along, and for 60,000 words, Z and Q just look slightly weird and dotted.

34:53.360 --> 34:53.940
You're used to it.

34:54.100 --> 34:58.620
And then suddenly, in the climactic final chapters of the thriller, the dam breaks.

34:59.040 --> 35:04.960
E, T, and A are finally unleashed, and they just absolutely warp into their final forms in the span of three pages.

35:05.180 --> 35:07.940
Because you're hitting their exposure targets every single paragraph.

35:08.320 --> 35:10.720
The visual texture of the book would be highly dynamic.

35:11.280 --> 35:14.880
The end of the book would feel vastly, aggressively different from the beginning.

35:15.500 --> 35:16.200
It really would.

35:16.200 --> 35:19.140
It also points to a fascinating psychological side effect.

35:19.840 --> 35:23.820
The reader's final knowledge of the new alphabet will be incredibly unbalanced.

35:24.240 --> 35:24.960
What do you mean by that?

35:25.480 --> 35:33.340
Well, by the time you finish the final chapter, you will have practiced reading the new alien shape for E thousands of times in its final form.

35:33.780 --> 35:37.060
It will be hardwired into your visual word form area.

35:37.240 --> 35:37.780
Oh, sure.

35:38.180 --> 35:42.440
But you might have only encountered the final shape for Z a couple of dozen times.

35:42.440 --> 35:47.560
You will have achieved fluency in the vowels, but you'll still be stuttering over the rare consonants.

35:48.000 --> 35:48.940
That makes a lot of sense.

35:49.680 --> 35:55.660
Any experimental test of this drifting method would have to rigorously measure retention based on these letter frequencies.

35:56.220 --> 36:02.180
Now, this is all assuming we are just swapping Latin letters for other Latin letters within the same structural rules.

36:02.340 --> 36:02.480
Right.

36:02.480 --> 36:03.920
But the paper doesn't stop there.

36:03.920 --> 36:10.640
It takes this theory and pushes it to the absolute limit by applying it to entirely different writing systems.

36:11.000 --> 36:12.100
Scripts in contact.

36:12.560 --> 36:13.160
Exactly.

36:13.940 --> 36:19.580
What happens when we try to drift English into Standard Galactic or even more complex into Arabic?

36:19.580 --> 36:22.000
Well, let's look at Standard Galactic first.

36:22.320 --> 36:23.220
Okay, what is that?

36:23.620 --> 36:26.100
It acts as a great baseline for cross-script drifting.

36:26.800 --> 36:35.220
It's an artificial script created in the 1990s for the Commander Keen video games and later popularized as the enchanting table language in Minecraft.

36:35.740 --> 36:36.660
Oh, I know the one.

36:37.140 --> 36:41.420
Structurally, it is a direct one-to-one visual substitution for the Latin alphabet.

36:42.200 --> 36:43.680
One symbol equals one letter.

36:44.460 --> 36:45.800
But it lacks a crucial feature.

36:46.100 --> 36:48.160
It has no upper and lowercase distinction.

36:48.580 --> 36:49.280
Oh, interesting.

36:49.580 --> 36:55.160
So, a capital A and a lowercase a map to the exact same blocky galactic symbol.

36:55.540 --> 36:55.940
Yes.

36:56.600 --> 37:00.960
So, if we drift a novel into Standard Galactic, the reader isn't just learning new shapes.

37:01.540 --> 37:03.860
They are actively losing orthographic information.

37:04.160 --> 37:04.380
Right.

37:04.380 --> 37:09.780
They lose capital letters as visual cues for identifying proper names or the start of a new sentence.

37:09.960 --> 37:12.400
They lose an entire layer of semantic metadata.

37:13.440 --> 37:17.880
And beyond that, the physical shapespaces of Latin and Galactic are entirely unrelated.

37:18.320 --> 37:19.460
They don't look anything alike.

37:19.460 --> 37:19.740
No.

37:19.960 --> 37:22.560
Galactic symbols are blocky alien runes.

37:23.160 --> 37:32.040
You can't just tell an open-type variable font to draw a straight mathematical line between a curved Latin S and a jagged galactic rune.

37:32.300 --> 37:32.840
Why not?

37:33.400 --> 37:38.200
A pure algorithmic interpolation would just create illegible tangled vector squiggles.

37:38.200 --> 37:41.180
The computer doesn't know how to gracefully connect the dots.

37:41.740 --> 37:42.080
Exactly.

37:42.480 --> 37:48.760
So, the paper proposes stepping away from pure automation and using hand-designed keyframes.

37:48.980 --> 37:49.260
Okay.

37:49.260 --> 38:01.100
A human typographer has to intervene and design one or two intermediate hybrid forms that carefully share features of both the Latin and the galactic scripts.

38:01.440 --> 38:08.360
You dye the engine through a curated path rather than letting the math tear the shapes apart.

38:08.360 --> 38:12.620
Which brings us to the ultimate stress test of this theory.

38:13.500 --> 38:13.800
Arabic.

38:14.620 --> 38:15.140
Oh, boy.

38:15.680 --> 38:16.280
Yes.

38:16.680 --> 38:20.960
And I feel like we have to tread incredibly carefully here.

38:20.960 --> 38:27.180
You can't just slap an Arabic aesthetic onto English letters and call it a day, right?

38:27.420 --> 38:28.020
Definitely not.

38:28.500 --> 38:33.300
The paper makes a very firm distinction between transliteration and re-glyphing.

38:33.520 --> 38:34.020
Okay.

38:34.080 --> 38:39.640
This is a vital point about respecting the structural integrity of living writing systems.

38:39.900 --> 38:40.740
What's the difference?

38:40.740 --> 38:49.800
Transliteration is changing the script to preserve pronunciation across languages, like trying to write English words using Arabic phonetic letters.

38:50.080 --> 38:50.240
Right.

38:51.060 --> 38:58.900
Re-glyphing, which is what our drifting book does, is changing the visual shapes but keeping the underlying language and spelling assignments the same.

38:59.480 --> 39:03.740
It's like drawing a Latin A but using Arabic-inspired calligraphy strokes.

39:04.020 --> 39:04.420
Exactly.

39:04.420 --> 39:12.600
The paper strongly cautions against treating Arabic merely as an ornamental resource, a visual skin you can just drape over English.

39:13.080 --> 39:18.680
Because Arabic isn't just a different font, it is structurally, fundamentally different from Latin.

39:19.260 --> 39:19.900
It really is.

39:20.180 --> 39:22.140
It's written right to left, for starters.

39:22.700 --> 39:23.400
It's cursive.

39:24.060 --> 39:30.780
And perhaps most importantly, the shape of a single letter physically changes depending on its position inside a word.

39:30.780 --> 39:34.740
This is the concept of contextual joining structures.

39:35.300 --> 39:35.880
How does that work?

39:36.480 --> 39:41.400
In Latin, a B looks like a B, whether it's at the beginning, middle, or end of a word.

39:42.100 --> 39:46.120
But a single Arabic letter can have up to four completely different forms.

39:46.840 --> 39:49.460
Isolated, initial, medial, and final.

39:49.980 --> 39:50.340
Wow.

39:50.740 --> 39:58.880
Its physical shape dynamically morphs depending on whether it needs to physically connect to the letter before it, the letter after it, both, or neither.

39:58.880 --> 40:07.160
So, if I try to build a cipher, and I naively swap an isolated Latin letter with a medial Arabic letter, what happens?

40:07.720 --> 40:09.940
You completely break the structural physics of the word.

40:10.460 --> 40:11.040
Break it how?

40:11.640 --> 40:14.120
The paper formulates this as a mathematical law.

40:14.820 --> 40:22.380
In a system with context-dependent shaping, substitution and rendering only commute if the substitution preserves joining classes.

40:22.740 --> 40:24.020
Let's translate that.

40:24.560 --> 40:26.100
What is a joining class?

40:26.100 --> 40:29.880
In Arabic, letters belong to different classes based on how they connect.

40:30.660 --> 40:31.900
Many letters are dual joining.

40:32.280 --> 40:35.220
They reach out and connect to letters on both their right and left sides.

40:35.280 --> 40:36.020
Okay, that makes sense.

40:36.200 --> 40:40.040
But some letters like ra or waw are right joining only.

40:40.520 --> 40:48.040
They connect to the letter before them, but they refuse to connect to the letter after them, creating a physical gap in the cursive word.

40:48.040 --> 40:59.060
Oh. So, if my cipher takes a dual joining letter, let's say noon, which holds hands with everyone, and swaps it for a right joining letter, like ra, I haven't just changed a shape.

40:59.160 --> 40:59.740
No, you haven't.

40:59.960 --> 41:03.720
I have fundamentally altered the orthographic behavior of the surrounding letters.

41:03.720 --> 41:06.060
The cursive line violently snaps.

41:06.440 --> 41:08.320
The word shatters into separate pieces.

41:08.940 --> 41:09.280
Exactly.

41:10.240 --> 41:12.560
A designer cannot just ignore the physics of the script.

41:12.920 --> 41:19.100
If you want to build a drifting hybrid between Latin and Arabic, you have to answer impossible structural questions.

41:19.420 --> 41:19.820
Like what?

41:20.340 --> 41:23.920
Is the Latin alphabet suddenly going to adopt cursive connecting strokes?

41:24.560 --> 41:27.060
Are we going to force English to be read right to left?

41:27.060 --> 41:29.380
You aren't just changing the paint on the walls.

41:29.500 --> 41:32.680
You are ripping out the structural engineering of the language's foundation.

41:33.380 --> 41:36.940
Which proves just how deeply intertwined a script is with the mechanics of reading.

41:37.380 --> 41:39.540
It's not just a collection of arbitrary pictures.

41:40.120 --> 41:42.060
It is an interlocking system of rules.

41:42.480 --> 41:48.680
And trying to mathematically describe those rules brings us to the deepest, most abstract, and frankly, most brain-melting part of the paper.

41:48.800 --> 41:49.840
The math gets intense here.

41:50.060 --> 41:55.520
Phleoxis uses three highly advanced branches of formal mathematics to interpret what a drifting book actually is.

41:55.520 --> 42:00.360
We are talking about category theory, type theory, and sheaf theory.

42:00.600 --> 42:01.720
Let's take it one by one.

42:01.820 --> 42:01.960
Okay.

42:02.340 --> 42:03.100
Category theory.

42:03.460 --> 42:08.600
Category theory is a branch of mathematics that deals with abstract structures, transformations, and compositions.

42:09.000 --> 42:13.620
In the context of our book, it looks at the pathways the letters take as they morph.

42:13.760 --> 42:13.960
Right.

42:14.420 --> 42:19.960
It proves that changing the topology of the routes in shape space is mostly just a metric problem.

42:20.160 --> 42:21.100
Okay, let's ground that.

42:21.480 --> 42:22.540
What does it mean for us?

42:22.540 --> 42:30.140
It means that mathematically, the exact path a letter takes as it morphs doesn't matter as much as maintaining the distance between the letters.

42:30.340 --> 42:30.700
Oh, I see.

42:31.100 --> 42:44.440
The category theory perspective proves that as long as we maintain that margin, as long as we use our detours and dots so the letters never collide, the specific geometry of the morph is secondary to the preservation of difference.

42:44.440 --> 42:48.360
It's a metric problem of distance, not a topological problem of exact routing.

42:48.620 --> 42:48.980
Exactly.

42:49.200 --> 42:50.280
Okay, I can grasp that.

42:50.720 --> 42:54.040
Keep the cars from hitting each other, and the exact route they take doesn't matter.

42:54.620 --> 42:55.660
Next is type theory.

42:55.960 --> 42:56.860
Right, type theory.

42:57.020 --> 42:58.200
This isn't about typography.

42:58.480 --> 43:00.280
It's about logic and computer programming.

43:00.740 --> 43:03.240
How does type theory define the publishing system?

43:03.660 --> 43:06.860
This is a brilliant concept from a software engineering perspective.

43:06.860 --> 43:10.400
In traditional software, a compiler checks your code.

43:10.980 --> 43:15.180
If you make a syntax error, the compiler throws an error and refuses to build the program.

43:15.800 --> 43:15.920
Right.

43:16.340 --> 43:21.700
The paper argues that a book builder shouldn't just be a dumb printing press that accepts whatever you feed it.

43:21.900 --> 43:24.700
The software system itself should be strictly typed.

43:25.020 --> 43:25.500
Meaning what?

43:25.780 --> 43:34.100
It should require a mathematical proof, what the paper calls a margin certificate, before it even allows the book to be exported to a PDF or an EPUB.

43:34.100 --> 43:35.860
That is incredible.

43:36.480 --> 43:43.740
The software reads the entire novelty schedule, calculates the multidimensional distances between all the morphing shapes on every single page,

43:44.140 --> 43:50.080
and if it detects that B and P are going to get too close and violate the margin of legibility on page 150,

43:50.360 --> 43:50.780
Yes.

43:50.940 --> 43:52.600
the compiler throws a fatal error.

43:53.120 --> 43:55.840
It physically refuses to build the book.

43:56.120 --> 44:00.540
The book has to mathematically prove its own legibility before it is allowed to exist.

44:00.540 --> 44:05.320
It turns typographical legibility from a subjective art into a provable theorem.

44:05.920 --> 44:06.920
But then...

44:06.920 --> 44:07.300
Oh no.

44:07.540 --> 44:09.460
...we arrive at sheaf theory.

44:09.880 --> 44:10.280
Yes.

44:10.980 --> 44:11.820
Sheaf theory.

44:12.240 --> 44:15.220
This is the part of the paper that genuinely broke my brain.

44:15.480 --> 44:19.900
It deals with the conflict between local readability and global convention.

44:20.300 --> 44:22.540
Walk us through the swap-ending paradox.

44:22.920 --> 44:25.920
To understand sheaf theory, let's use a spatial analogy.

44:26.580 --> 44:29.020
Imagine you're standing in a large, flat field.

44:29.020 --> 44:31.020
Okay. I'm in a field.

44:31.420 --> 44:35.460
Locally, from your specific viewpoint, the Earth appears completely flat.

44:36.380 --> 44:38.580
The geometry works. You can build a house on it.

44:38.740 --> 44:38.940
Sure.

44:39.320 --> 44:43.580
But globally, if you zoom out into space, the Earth is a sphere.

44:44.340 --> 44:46.480
Sheaf theory is a mathematical tool that asks,

44:46.940 --> 44:49.040
if I have a collection of local observations,

44:49.860 --> 44:54.640
can I glue them all together to form a coherent, contradiction-free global structure?

44:54.640 --> 44:59.540
And when we apply that to our drifting book, the answer is a horrifying no.

45:00.480 --> 45:00.960
Exactly.

45:01.800 --> 45:03.440
Let's look at our reciprocal cipher.

45:04.060 --> 45:06.340
A swaps to B, and B swaps to A.

45:06.820 --> 45:09.400
You open the book to page one, it's written in normal English.

45:09.780 --> 45:09.960
Right.

45:10.420 --> 45:15.380
In this local space, the physical shape B means the phonetic sound B.

45:15.580 --> 45:16.460
Makes perfect sense.

45:16.460 --> 45:18.680
Now skip to the final chapter of the book.

45:19.340 --> 45:22.020
The script is completely drifted to its final cipher state.

45:22.680 --> 45:25.120
In this new local space, because of the swap,

45:25.440 --> 45:29.280
the physical shape B now represents the phonetic sound A.

45:29.840 --> 45:30.360
Wait.

45:30.860 --> 45:33.740
If A swapped to B and B swapped to A,

45:34.080 --> 45:38.340
doesn't that mean by the end of the book I'm reading the exact same shapes I started with,

45:38.440 --> 45:40.060
but with totally backward meanings?

45:40.680 --> 45:41.120
Yep.

45:41.120 --> 45:44.460
How does my brain not just completely short-circuit?

45:44.920 --> 45:50.100
Because sheaf theory proves the book is locally coherent, but globally incoherent.

45:50.300 --> 45:50.560
Meaning?

45:51.000 --> 45:55.760
If you look at any single local window of text, say, just reading chapter 10,

45:56.880 --> 46:02.420
the drift from page to page is so infinitesimally tiny that the text functions perfectly.

46:02.540 --> 46:03.080
You can read it.

46:03.340 --> 46:06.060
The meanings of the shapes are consistent within that small window.

46:06.240 --> 46:07.440
The local rules work.

46:07.440 --> 46:11.160
But if you zoom out and try to find a global rule?

46:11.660 --> 46:12.900
The global structure collapses.

46:13.780 --> 46:18.080
There is no single global key or decoder ring that covers the entire book.

46:18.600 --> 46:20.120
If I hand you this book and ask,

46:20.600 --> 46:22.880
what sound does the shape B make?

46:23.540 --> 46:24.840
There is no factual answer.

46:25.280 --> 46:27.340
Because it means B on page 1.

46:27.760 --> 46:31.740
It means some weird unpronounceable hybrid on page 150.

46:32.040 --> 46:34.520
It means A on page 300.

46:35.060 --> 46:36.940
You cannot glue the local data together.

46:37.540 --> 46:40.040
It's exactly like an M.C. Escher staircase painting.

46:40.360 --> 46:41.520
Oh, that's a great comparison.

46:41.780 --> 46:43.920
The one with the monks walking in an infinite square.

46:44.340 --> 46:48.760
If you isolate any single local step on that staircase, it makes perfect physical sense.

46:48.920 --> 46:51.060
The foot goes up to the next step.

46:51.220 --> 46:51.940
Gravity works.

46:52.240 --> 46:52.500
Yeah.

46:52.840 --> 46:55.380
But globally, if you follow the whole staircase around,

46:55.480 --> 46:58.240
you end up back at the exact same vertical height you started at.

46:58.240 --> 47:03.280
The local physics work perfectly, but the global geometry is an impossible paradox.

47:03.840 --> 47:06.700
That is the perfect visualization of the swap ending paradox.

47:06.940 --> 47:11.780
The reader's brain is successfully navigating the Escher staircase because the reader is

47:11.780 --> 47:15.580
maintaining a convention that varies entirely with the phase of the book.

47:15.960 --> 47:16.260
Wow.

47:16.540 --> 47:22.200
Without consciously realizing it, you are holding multiple conflicting definitions of the exact

47:22.200 --> 47:27.720
same shape in your working memory and switching between them depending on which chapter you are in.

47:27.720 --> 47:31.720
But relying on unconscious phase shifting seems incredibly fragile.

47:31.720 --> 47:35.560
What if I put the book down for a week and then open it back up to the middle?

47:35.560 --> 47:36.540
You'd be lost.

47:37.080 --> 47:38.720
I've lost the momentum of the drift.

47:39.260 --> 47:42.080
The paper suggests solving this by adding a phase marker, right?

47:42.440 --> 47:42.720
Yes.

47:43.040 --> 47:47.600
Because there is no global key, the brain needs a contextual anchor.

47:48.160 --> 47:52.820
The paper suggests an environmental cue, like making the header of the page or the background

47:52.820 --> 47:57.100
color slowly gradient from blue on page one to red on page 300.

47:57.100 --> 48:02.400
So if I open the book randomly and see a purple header, my subconscious brain immediately knows,

48:02.800 --> 48:05.220
ah, we are in the 50% hybrid zone.

48:05.600 --> 48:09.100
I need to load the purple dictionary into my visual word form area.

48:09.420 --> 48:09.820
Exactly.

48:10.420 --> 48:15.520
It formalizes the difference between temporarily understanding a passage through heavy contextual

48:15.520 --> 48:19.840
assistance and truly holding a stable orthographic convention in your mind.

48:19.840 --> 48:21.380
That's fascinating.

48:22.200 --> 48:26.480
The drifting book is asking your brain to constantly, dynamically overwrite your most

48:26.480 --> 48:29.220
deeply practiced hardwired associations.

48:29.760 --> 48:35.640
And the fact that the brain can do it receptively without flashcards brings us to the grand takeaway

48:35.640 --> 48:37.080
of this entire concept.

48:37.300 --> 48:38.960
The unconscious lexicon.

48:39.360 --> 48:39.640
Right.

48:39.940 --> 48:43.600
A publication, a printed book does not have to be a static dead object.

48:43.760 --> 48:45.380
It does not have to be fixed architecture.

48:45.600 --> 48:46.360
It really doesn't.

48:46.360 --> 48:50.180
It can be designed as a passage between representational conventions.

48:50.180 --> 48:56.480
It can be a cognitive machine that actively alters your neurological hardware while you are

48:56.480 --> 48:58.860
happily distracted by the software of the story.

48:59.480 --> 49:01.240
It entirely shifts the burden of learning.

49:02.080 --> 49:07.240
Instead of productive acquisition, you know, you sitting at a desk with a pencil, forcing yourself

49:07.240 --> 49:12.080
to memorize a shape through sheer willpower, it relies on the environment to do the work.

49:12.420 --> 49:14.500
The book manages the novelty load.

49:14.500 --> 49:17.580
The book paces the exposure based on Zipf's law.

49:17.980 --> 49:21.620
The book uses metric detours to bypass your evolutionary confusion.

49:22.220 --> 49:25.120
It slips the new shapes past your biological defenses.

49:25.780 --> 49:30.560
And that leads to a deeply provocative thought that I want to leave you, the listener, with today.

49:30.680 --> 49:31.660
Oh, this is a good one.

49:32.160 --> 49:36.760
Because Blightzing's paper is strictly focused on typography, alphabets, and the mechanics

49:36.760 --> 49:37.220
of reading.

49:37.420 --> 49:38.920
But let's extrapolate this.

49:38.980 --> 49:40.880
Let's connect the dots to the modern world.

49:41.020 --> 49:41.280
Okay.

49:41.280 --> 49:46.100
If all brains can be so smoothly tricked into learning an entirely new alphabet, literally

49:46.100 --> 49:51.600
overwriting decades of hardwired survival habits, just by tweaking the novelty load and

49:51.600 --> 49:56.920
exploiting exposure frequencies in a single novel, what else are we passively absorbing?

49:57.440 --> 50:02.680
If you connect this to the bigger picture, it raises terrifying questions about the structural

50:02.680 --> 50:04.220
design of our digital environments.

50:04.220 --> 50:04.820
Exactly.

50:05.540 --> 50:10.660
If variable fonts can drift our linguistic hardware 1% at a time, how are the invisible

50:10.660 --> 50:15.440
schedules and algorithms of our daily social media feeds drifting our actual cognitive

50:15.440 --> 50:15.940
habits?

50:16.220 --> 50:17.120
That's a scary thought.

50:17.740 --> 50:18.740
What social conventions?

50:18.980 --> 50:20.160
What behavioral norms?

50:20.340 --> 50:26.200
What definitions of reality are platforms slowly morphing pixel by pixel without us ever

50:26.200 --> 50:27.040
seeing the transition?

50:27.040 --> 50:32.700
In the book, we rely on the type theory compiler to throw an error if the margin of legibility

50:32.700 --> 50:33.560
drops to zero.

50:34.220 --> 50:38.540
But in our digital lives, we don't have a margin certificate to warn us when our perception

50:38.540 --> 50:42.200
of reality is about to collide and become indistinguishable from fiction.

50:42.400 --> 50:43.040
We really don't.

50:43.660 --> 50:46.680
We are experiencing a global drift in how we process information.

50:47.140 --> 50:51.320
And there are no blue to red phase markers at the top of our screens to tell us which version

50:51.320 --> 50:52.960
of reality we are currently reading.

50:52.960 --> 50:56.500
We are the frog in the algorithmic boiling water.

50:57.320 --> 51:00.500
Think about that next time you feel like the world is shifting right under your eyes.

51:01.040 --> 51:03.840
The typographical quicksand isn't just in experimental books.

51:04.400 --> 51:06.720
It's in the feed you scroll every single day.

51:07.220 --> 51:09.140
Let that sit in your brain for a while.

51:09.700 --> 51:10.700
That's it for this deep dive.

51:11.120 --> 51:11.740
See you next time.

